 "bloques": [
  {
   "tipo": "explicacion",
   "titulo": "Have got to",
   "texto": [
    {
     "@es": "**Have to** y **have got to** significan «tener que»; **have got to** es más informal. Con **he**, **she** o **it**, usa **has got to**: **She has got to sign this form**.",
     "@en": "**Have to** and **have got to** mean «have to»; **have got to** is more informal. With **he**, **she** or **it**, use **has got to**: **She has got to sign this form**."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "tipo": "explicacion",
   "titulo": {
    "@es": "Need y needn't",
    "@en": "Need and don't"
   },
   "texto": [
    {
     "@es": "Como verbo principal, **need** suele ir con **to**: **Do I need to sign?** **Needn't + verbo base** (sin **to**) significa «no hace falta» y equivale a **don't have to**: **You needn't come**. **Mustn't** significa «no debes; está prohibido», no falta de necesidad.",
     "@en": "As the main verb, **need** usually goes with **to**: **Do I need to sign?** **Needn't + verbo base** (without **to**) means «I don't have to.» and equals **don't have to**: **You needn't come**. **Mustn't** means «You must not; it is forbidden», no lack of need."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "tipo": "explicacion",
   "titulo": {
    "@es": "Fee y tax",
    "@en": "Fee and tax"
   },
   "texto": [
    {
     "@es": "**Fee** = tasa o cuota que se paga por un servicio. **Tax** = impuesto. No significan lo mismo.",
     "@en": "**Fee** = fee or fee paid for a service. **Tax** = tax. They don't mean the same thing."
    }
   ]
  },
